# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sharpline — example environment
#
#   cp .env.example .env && make up
#
# `.env` is git-ignored and MUST NEVER be committed. This file is the committed
# template: every value here is a safe, non-secret local default, and the stack
# comes up with the file copied verbatim.
#
# ── HOW THIS FILE IS ACTUALLY READ ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The Makefile passes `--env-file $(ROOT_DIR)/.env` to every `docker compose`
# invocation, and compose.yaml additionally declares `env_file: ../../.env` on
# every service. Those are two DIFFERENT mechanisms and the difference explains
# most of the notes below:
#
#   1. INTERPOLATION — `--env-file .env` supplies the values for every
#      `${VAR}` / `${VAR:-default}` written inside the compose YAML.
#      Compose is NOT given the repo-root .env automatically: the compose files
#      live in deploy/compose/, and compose looks for `.env` next to the compose
#      file, not at the project root. The Makefile's `--env-file` flag is what
#      wires it up. Running `docker compose ...` by hand without that flag
#      silently falls back to every `:-default` in the YAML.
#
#   2. CONTAINER ENVIRONMENT — `env_file:` loads EVERY key in this file into
#      EVERY container. A service's own `environment:` block then overrides any
#      key it also sets. So a value here that compose also sets explicitly is
#      documentation of the config surface, not a live knob — those are marked
#      "COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS".
#
# ── WHAT VALIDATES THIS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# internal/platform/config reads SHARPLINE_ENV, SHARPLINE_LOG_LEVEL,
# SHARPLINE_HTTP_ADDR, SHARPLINE_POSTGRES_DSN, SHARPLINE_REDIS_ADDR,
# SHARPLINE_REDIS_PASSWORD, SHARPLINE_KAFKA_BROKERS, SHARPLINE_JWT_SIGNING_KEY,
# SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT, ODDS_API_KEY, SHARPLINE_SYNTHETIC_SEED,
# SHARPLINE_INGEST_LIVE_INTERVAL and SHARPLINE_PRICER_REFERENCE_BOOKS — into a
# typed struct at startup, collects every
# problem, and fails the process with all of them at once. A bad value is a
# hard, immediate, loud failure and never a silent fallback (CLAUDE.md §12).
# Everything else below is consumed by a container image, by compose, or by
# Caddy.
#
# Placeholders marked CHANGE-ME are fine for a laptop and are NOT fine anywhere
# reachable from a network. In Kubernetes these come from Secrets (sealed-secrets
# or SOPS), never from a file like this one.
#
# ── SUPPLIED BY THE ENVIRONMENT, NOT BY THIS FILE ────────────────────────────
#
# Three variables are interpolated by the compose files and are deliberately NOT
# assigned here. Setting them here would be inert at best and misleading at
# worst: for interpolation the shell environment outranks `--env-file`, so the
# real source always wins.
#
#   HOME           compose.tools.yaml mounts ${HOME:-/tmp}/.kube into the tools
#                  container so `make tf-*` and kubectl see your kubeconfig.
#                  Your shell sets it.
#
#   CI             compose.tools.yaml passes ${CI:-} to Playwright, which uses
#                  it to choose its reporter and retry policy. GitHub Actions
#                  sets it; locally it is correctly empty.
#
#   DOCKER_SOCKET  Path to the host Docker socket, bind-mounted into the tools
#                  container so testcontainers-go can spawn siblings. The
#                  Makefile DISCOVERS it with `docker context inspect` and
#                  exports it, because it is NOT always /var/run/docker.sock —
#                  with Docker Desktop's "Allow the default Docker socket to be
#                  used" turned off it lives at ~/.docker/run/docker.sock, and a
#                  hardcoded path silently breaks `make test`. Override for one
#                  run with `make test DOCKER_SOCKET=/path/to/docker.sock`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Deployment environment. Selects logging format, whether dev-only affordances
# are permitted (the proxy serves /_kafka-ui, /grafana, /prometheus and /jaeger
# ONLY when this is `dev`, and 404s them otherwise), and whether TLS is
# internal-CA or real.
#
# MUST be exactly one of: dev | test | staging | prod
# Anything else — including `local` — is a hard startup failure in
# internal/platform/config, not a silent fallback: "prod" vs "prd" deciding
# whether debug endpoints are exposed is precisely the class of bug that
# fail-fast validation exists to prevent.
SHARPLINE_ENV=dev

# Minimum log level for the log/slog JSON handler.
# One of: debug | info | warn | error
#
# COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS per service: compose.yaml resolves
# ${SHARPLINE_LOG_LEVEL:-info}, and the dev overlay (compose.dev.yaml, applied
# by `make up`) resolves ${SHARPLINE_LOG_LEVEL:-debug}. Editing it here DOES
# take effect, because both of those read this value first.
SHARPLINE_LOG_LEVEL=debug

# host:port each Go service binds its HTTP server to — /healthz, /readyz,
# /metrics, and (for `api`) the REST surface, (for `stream`) the WS upgrade.
#
# Bind inside the container only. The container sits on an internal bridge
# network and nothing but the proxy publishes a host port (CLAUDE.md §9, §12).
#
# COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS. It is per-service, and compose.yaml sets it explicitly
# on each one. Editing it here has no effect under `make up`; it is listed so
# the config surface is complete and so a binary run outside compose has a
# value. The frozen internal port map is:
#
#     api 8080 | stream 8081 | pricer 8082 | ingest 8083 | settle 8084
#
SHARPLINE_HTTP_ADDR=:8080


# ── Postgres 17 + TimescaleDB ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# THESE THREE ARE LIVE and are the ones to edit. They are consumed directly by
# the timescale/timescaledb image's entrypoint to initialize the cluster on
# first boot (these are the upstream image's own variable names, not ours), AND
# compose composes SHARPLINE_POSTGRES_DSN out of them for every Go service, the
# goose migration runner, and the psql shell. Changing the password here
# therefore changes it in exactly one place.
#
# The password is baked into the data volume on first boot. Changing it later
# requires `make down-v` (which wipes volumes) or an ALTER ROLE by hand.
POSTGRES_USER=sharpline
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME-postgres
POSTGRES_DB=sharpline

# Full libpq connection string used by every Go service and by `migrate`.
# `postgres` is the compose service name, resolved by the network's service-name
# DNS. sslmode=disable is correct on the internal bridge network and is NOT
# correct anywhere else.
#
# COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS: compose.yaml builds the DSN from POSTGRES_USER /
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB above and sets it on each service explicitly.
# Keep it in sync with those three; it documents the config surface and is what
# a binary run outside compose would read.
SHARPLINE_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://sharpline:CHANGE-ME-postgres@postgres:5432/sharpline?sslmode=disable


# ── Redis 7 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# host:port of the Redis instance. Snapshot cache, WebSocket presence and
# subscription state, distributed rate limiting, idempotency keys.
# Never the source of truth — Redis may be flushed at any time without data loss.
#
# COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS with the service-name DNS value on the internal network.
SHARPLINE_REDIS_ADDR=redis:6379

# LIVE. Redis AUTH password. The redis container is started with --requirepass
# set from this same variable, so the two cannot drift; changing it here changes
# both. A mismatch would fail every client call with NOAUTH.
SHARPLINE_REDIS_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME-redis


# ── Apache Kafka (KRaft) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated bootstrap broker list for franz-go.
# Single-broker KRaft locally; a comma-separated list of StatefulSet pod DNS
# names in the cluster. No ZooKeeper anywhere — Kafka 4.x removed it entirely.
#
# Topics are declared in Terraform, never created by hand (CLAUDE.md §9):
#   odds.raw.{provider}  retention
#   odds.normalized      compacted, key = market_id
#   price.computed       compacted
#   wager.events         retention — the settlement audit trail
#
# COMPOSE OVERRIDES THIS with the service-name DNS value.
SHARPLINE_KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka:9092

# LIVE. KRaft cluster ID, consumed by the kafka container's formatter.
# Generated once and then FROZEN — changing it after the first boot makes the
# existing Kafka log directory unreadable and the broker refuses to start.
# `make down-v` (which wipes volumes) is the only safe way to change it.
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=5L6g3nShT-eMCtK--X86sw


# ── OpenTelemetry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# OTLP endpoint for the collector. Traces span ingest -> pricer -> stream so a
# single odds update can be followed end to end.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY. Empty is not "unconfigured", it is the correct default, and
# the two compose layers cooperate to make it so:
#
#   compose.yaml      SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT: ${SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT:-}
#   compose.obs.yaml  SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT: ${SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT:-otel-collector:4317}
#
# `:-` substitutes when the variable is unset OR EMPTY, so an empty value here
# means: export disabled with the observability profile off, and automatically
# pointed at the collector the moment the `obs` profile brings it up. Hardcoding
# `otel-collector:4317` here instead would make every service dial a host that
# does not exist whenever obs is off.
#
# Set it explicitly only to send telemetry somewhere other than the bundled
# collector. Use gRPC port 4317 (HTTP/protobuf is 4318 if you switch exporters).
SHARPLINE_OTEL_ENDPOINT=


# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# LIVE, and read only from this file — compose never sets it, so if it is
# missing here `api` fails to start. Symmetric signing key for JWT access
# tokens. Rotating refresh tokens with reuse detection are stored server-side
# and are not derived from this value.
#
# Startup validation rejects anything shorter than 32 bytes. Generate a real one
# — 32 bytes of CSPRNG output, base64, produced in a container like everything
# else:
#     docker run --rm alpine sh -c 'head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64'
#
# Changing it invalidates every outstanding access token, which is the intended
# behaviour on compromise.
SHARPLINE_JWT_SIGNING_KEY=CHANGE-ME-generate-32-random-bytes-base64


# ── Odds provider ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# LIVE, and read only from this file — compose never sets it. API key for
# The Odds API (https://the-odds-api.com). See docs/adr/0003.
#
# THE EMPTY VALUE IS THE DEFAULT AND IS DELIBERATE:
#
#   EMPTY  -> `ingest` starts the SYNTHETIC provider: a stochastic market-maker
#             producing realistic line movement, steam moves and book
#             disagreement. This is the default, the CI path, the offline path,
#             and the correct choice for day-to-day development — the free
#             tier's 500 credits/month cannot drive a live board (ADR 0003).
#
#   SET    -> `ingest` starts the REAL adapter and polls The Odds API within the
#             configured quota budget.
#
# Both adapters sit behind the identical ProviderAdapter interface; nothing
# downstream of ingest can tell which one is running.
#
# THIS IS A SECRET. It goes in `.env` (git-ignored) locally and in a Kubernetes
# Secret in-cluster. It never goes in git, in a ConfigMap, or in an image layer.
ODDS_API_KEY=

# REQUIRED WHENEVER ODDS_API_KEY IS SET, and ignored entirely when it is not.
#
# The monthly credit budget the token-bucket limiter is sized against. CLAUDE.md
# §5 requires the provider quota be a config value, and internal/ingest/provider/
# theoddsapi refuses to start on a zero budget rather than silently spending an
# unknown allowance: `Limiter.MonthlyCredits must be positive`.
#
# THE FAILURE MODE THIS COMMENT EXISTS TO PREVENT: setting ODDS_API_KEY without
# this value makes `ingest` crash-loop while every other container reports
# healthy, so the stack looks up and the odds path is dead.
#
# 100000 is ADR 0003's recommended tier. Set it to the plan you actually bought;
# an over-stated budget spends real money and an under-stated one throttles.
SHARPLINE_ODDS_MONTHLY_CREDITS=100000

# OPTIONAL. The provider bookmaker key `ingest` marks as the sharp reference on
# every record it publishes. Defaults to "pinnacle"
# (theoddsapi.DefaultReferenceBook). The Odds API publishes no sharpness label,
# so this is OUR judgement and is recorded as such on every computed price.
SHARPLINE_ODDS_REFERENCE_BOOK=

# LIVE. Ordered, comma-separated list of book slugs `pricer` treats as the sharp
# reference, most preferred first.
#
# It is the FALLBACK, not the usual path: a book the provider designated on the
# wire outranks this list entirely, and both adapters designate one. It exists
# for a provider that designates nothing, and because sharpness is an opinion
# rather than a fact any provider reports (internal/pricing/reference.go).
#
# Empty is legal and means "provider designation only".
SHARPLINE_PRICER_REFERENCE_BOOKS=pinnacle,sharpline

# LIVE (compose resolves ${SHARPLINE_SYNTHETIC_SEED:-0}). Seed for the synthetic
# provider's RNG. A fixed seed makes the generated market behaviour fully
# reproducible, which is what makes integration tests deterministic. Change it
# to get a different but equally valid market. Must parse as a base-10 int64.
# Ignored entirely when ODDS_API_KEY is set.
SHARPLINE_SYNTHETIC_SEED=1


# ── Frontend (build- and run-time, baked into the client bundle) ─────────────
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into JavaScript shipped to the browser.
# NEVER put a secret in one. They are passed twice — as build args (so they are
# compiled into the client bundle) and as runtime env — and both reads come from
# the two variables below.

# LIVE. Base URL the browser uses for REST calls, and for the WebSocket stream.
#
# THESE ARE ROOT-RELATIVE ON PURPOSE. The proxy serves three hostnames
# (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, and whatever SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST names) over TLS,
# and redirects plain HTTP to HTTPS. A hardcoded absolute origin such as
# `http://localhost/api` therefore breaks two ways at once: browsing the app at
# https://sharpline.localhost would make every API call cross-origin, and the
# http:// scheme would be blocked as mixed content on an https page. A relative
# path inherits whichever scheme and host the page was actually loaded from, so
# it is correct for all three names and both schemes.
#
# Override with an absolute origin only when the API is genuinely served from a
# different host than the app (e.g. https://api.example.com), and set CORS
# accordingly. `ws://` / `wss://` for the socket in that case.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=/ws


# ── Proxy (Caddy — the only container with published host ports) ─────────────

# LIVE, and the ONE knob that changes the hostname the stack is served on.
# Read in two places that must agree, and both read this variable:
#
#   deploy/compose/compose.yaml  SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST: ${SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST:-sharpline.localhost}
#   deploy/proxy/Caddyfile       localhost, 127.0.0.1, {$SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST:sharpline.localhost} {
#
# Caddy serves `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` unconditionally, plus this third
# name. With the shipped `tls internal`, Caddy's own CA issues a short-lived
# certificate on startup: no ACME round-trip, no rate limits, works offline.
# Browsers warn until you trust the root at
# /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt on the host.
#
# Setting this to a real public domain makes Caddy attempt real certificate
# issuance, so DO NOT set one until the host is actually reachable on :80/:443,
# or you will burn Let's Encrypt rate limit on failed challenges.
#
# (An earlier revision of this file defined SHARPLINE_DOMAIN here. Nothing ever
# read it. It has been removed; this is the variable that works.)
#
# THE DEPLOYED VM uses sharpline.andrewpleeter.us, set in ITS OWN .env -- not
# here. This file stays on the localhost default because it is the template a
# contributor copies, and `make up` has to work with no domain and no DNS.
#
# That deployment sits behind Cloudflare, which changes what "TLS" means and is
# worth writing down because the two ends have to agree:
#
#   Cloudflare SSL mode      what the origin must serve
#   -----------------------  --------------------------------------------------
#   Flexible                 plain HTTP on :80  (what the Caddyfile ships today)
#   Full / Full (strict)     real HTTPS on :443 (uncomment `tls {$SHARPLINE_ACME_EMAIL}`)
#
# A mismatch is visible as a Cloudflare 5xx rather than anything in the logs
# here: 521 means Cloudflare could not reach the origin at all, 526 means it
# reached it but rejected the certificate.
SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST=sharpline.localhost

# Contact address for ACME registration, passed to the proxy container by
# compose (${SHARPLINE_ACME_EMAIL:-}) and read by the Caddyfile as
# {$SHARPLINE_ACME_EMAIL}.
#
# INERT WHILE THE CADDYFILE SAYS `tls internal`, which is the shipped default.
# It becomes live only when that one line is switched to `tls {$SHARPLINE_ACME_EMAIL}`
# for a real deployment. Empty is correct locally; the CA uses it for expiry
# notices, so set a real mailbox before going public.
SHARPLINE_ACME_EMAIL=


# ── Observability (the `obs` compose profile — off by default) ───────────────
#
# These reach a human only through the proxy: none of these containers publishes
# a host port either, and the proxy exposes their routes ONLY when
# SHARPLINE_ENV=dev. Start them with the obs profile, then browse
# https://localhost/grafana, /prometheus, /jaeger.

# LIVE. Grafana admin login. Dev-only credentials; in the cluster these come
# from a Secret. Grafana refuses to start with the password left as literal
# "admin".
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=sharpline-dev-grafana

# LIVE. Where Grafana believes it is mounted. Must match the proxy's subpath and
# the scheme/host you actually browse, or every generated link and asset URL
# breaks. https, not http: Caddy serves TLS and redirects plain HTTP, and an
# http root URL on an https page produces mixed-content failures. If you browse
# via SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST rather than localhost, change the host here to match.
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=https://localhost/grafana

# LIVE. Prometheus TSDB retention and external URL. 15d of local scrape data is
# plenty for the odds-staleness SLO dashboard and keeps the volume small.
# Same scheme/host rule as GRAFANA_ROOT_URL.
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION=15d
PROMETHEUS_EXTERNAL_URL=https://localhost/prometheus

# LIVE. Jaeger in-memory trace cap. All-in-one stores traces in RAM; raising
# this raises the container's memory ceiling proportionally.
JAEGER_MAX_TRACES=50000


# ── Test and load tooling (one-shot containers, `tools` profile) ─────────────

# LIVE. Base URL Playwright drives. Always the proxy — E2E must exercise the
# same entrypoint a real browser uses, not a service's own container port.
# `proxy` is the compose service name, resolved by the internal network's DNS.
#
# ─ KNOWN GAP, measured against a running stack, do not "fix" it here ─
# Caddy serves three host names and `proxy` is not one of them, so a request
# with `Host: proxy` matches no site block:
#
#   http://proxy/    -> 308 Location: https://proxy/   (Caddy's automatic
#                       HTTP->HTTPS redirect fires for any host)
#   https://proxy/   -> TLS alert internal error — the internal CA issued no
#                       certificate for the name `proxy`
#
# The same request with SNI/Host `localhost` or `sharpline.localhost`, resolved
# to the proxy container's address, returns 200. So the value below is right and
# the missing piece is a name the network can resolve to the proxy: either a
# compose network alias for SHARPLINE_PROXY_HOST on the proxy service, or `proxy`
# added to the Caddyfile's site address list. Until one of those lands, E2E and
# load runs cannot reach the app, and changing this variable alone cannot fix it.
PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL=http://proxy

# LIVE. Locust distributed load generation, targeting WebSocket fanout.
# The charter's stated goal is 10k concurrent subscribers on one node; reaching
# it means raising LOCUST_USERS and scaling worker replicas, since each Locust
# user is a Python greenlet rather than a goroutine (CLAUDE.md §10).
# LOCUST_EXPECT_WORKERS must match the replica count `make load` starts, or the
# master waits forever for workers that never arrive.
# LOCUST_TARGET carries the same known gap documented under PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL
# above: `Host: proxy` matches no Caddy site block yet.
LOCUST_TARGET=http://proxy
LOCUST_USERS=1000
LOCUST_SPAWN_RATE=50
LOCUST_RUN_TIME=5m
LOCUST_EXPECT_WORKERS=4

# LIVE. testcontainers-go, used by `make test`. The test container talks to the
# host Docker daemon through the mounted socket and spawns SIBLING containers;
# the host override is how those siblings are reached from inside the test
# container. Ryuk is the reaper that cleans them up — disable it only if your
# environment forbids privileged containers, and clean up by hand if you do.
TESTCONTAINERS_HOST_OVERRIDE=host.docker.internal
TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED=false

# LIVE. Terraform environment selector — the tools container runs with
# working_dir deploy/terraform/envs/${TF_ENV}. `local` is the kind cluster;
# `prod` is the real target.
TF_ENV=local

# LIVE (compose resolves ${SHARPLINE_INGEST_LIVE_INTERVAL:-}). Poll cadence for
# the LIVE window only -- events that are in play. A Go duration ("90s", "5m").
# Empty means the scheduler's own default of 90s, which is the number ADR 0003's
# quota arithmetic is built on: the live tier is roughly 77% of the monthly
# credit spend, so halving this roughly doubles the bill.
#
# It must be positive. A zero or negative value is REFUSED at startup rather than
# clamped, because a non-positive poll interval is an unbounded loop against a
# metered API and a silently-corrected cadence is a bill nobody predicted.
#
# Setting it below the synthetic provider's 10s step is how you observe change
# detection doing its job: consecutive polls inside one step return identical
# payloads, which the fingerprint suppresses instead of republishing.
SHARPLINE_INGEST_LIVE_INTERVAL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHARPLINE_TOTP_KEYRING -- AEAD keyring sealing TOTP shared secrets AT REST.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format is auth.ParseKeyring's:  id:base64key[,id:base64key...]
# The FIRST entry is the active key; the rest are kept for decryption only.
# The key is base64 of exactly 32 bytes. Generate one in a container:
#
#     docker run --rm alpine sh -c 'head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64'
#
# THIS IS NOT THE JWT KEY AND MUST NOT BE THE SAME VALUE. The JWT key signs
# short-lived bearer tokens and can be rotated freely -- everything signed under
# the old one expires within minutes. THIS key DECRYPTS DATA AT REST: lose it
# and every enrolled second factor becomes permanently unopenable, locking those
# users out of their accounts. Rotate by PREPENDING a new key and keeping the
# old one in the list until every row has been resealed.
#
# OPTIONAL. Empty means TOTP enrolment is REFUSED rather than performed without
# encryption. Password login, refresh-token rotation and the entire read surface
# are unaffected.
SHARPLINE_TOTP_KEYRING=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHARPLINE_TRUSTED_PROXIES -- whose forwarding headers `api` may believe.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated CIDRs (a bare IP is accepted and means that single host).
#
# CLAUDE.md section 6 requires rate limiting per IP, and a per-IP limiter is only
# as good as its idea of who the client is. Behind the proxy, RemoteAddr is the
# PROXY, so:
#
#   unset          every request in the system buckets on one address and a
#                  single abusive client exhausts the limit for everybody.
#   set too wide   any caller picks its own bucket by sending a header, and the
#                  control stops existing entirely.
#
# So set it to EXACTLY the hop in front of the service and nothing else. Never a
# blanket private-range list: on a shared bridge network "trust RFC1918" means
# "trust every container", which is every container that could be compromised.
#
#   compose      the proxy's pinned address, 172.28.0.2/32 (see compose.yaml --
#                the subnet is pinned so this address is stable)
#   Kubernetes   the ingress controller's pod CIDR
#
# The compose default is applied in compose.yaml, so leaving this empty is fine
# locally.
SHARPLINE_TRUSTED_PROXIES=
